event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1110,"item_id":"fc_031097c1e2840610016adb39536698819196cacb921ae8377f","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_input\\\":\\\"prior-candidate.json\\\",\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Fix r>0, J=[r/2,3r/2], and choose compact C from H003. For every s∈J, u(·,s)=q on the open complement of C. Smoothness implies every first and higher derivative in x and t vanishes there; consequently e and e_t at each fixed s are continuous and supported in C, hence compactly supported. M001 applies with X=ℝⁿ, Lebesgue measure and V=ℝ, since Euclidean opens are Borel measurable and Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets. Thus e(·,s), e_t(·,s), and all continuous compactly supported scalar fields below are integrable; E(s) is the ordinary finite Lebesgue integral. C×J is compact by M005, and e_t is continuous on it, so M004 gives uniform continuity. Apply M006 with α=ℝⁿ, μ=Lebesgue, 𝕜=ℝ, E=ℝ, x₀=r, s=(r/2,3r/2), F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ), F'(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ). The neighborhood inclusion holds; F(τ) is strongly measurable because it is continuous, and F(r) integrable by M001; F'(r) is strongly measurable. Continuity on compact C×J gives a finite bound K for |e_t| there. Set b=K·1_C; C is compact measurable of finite measure, so b is integrable. Outside C the derivative vanishes, and on C it is bounded by K; hence |F'(τ)(x)|≤b(x) for τ∈s. Smoothness gives the required HasDerivAt in τ for every x and τ. All M006 hypotheses are now checked, yielding E differentiable at r and E'(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx. For the later coordinate argument choose L bounding C: each coordinate projection of C is compact and therefore bounded; the finitely many coordinate bounds have a common finite maximum. The box [-L,L]^n contains C. C and J compact give C×J compact by M005; these are proved compactness facts, not properties granted by the witness declarations.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"For fixed r and each j define g_j(x)=u_t(x,r)·u_{x_j}(x,r). The factors and their derivatives vanish outside C, so g_j and ∂_j g_j are smooth and compactly supported; M001 proves their integrability. Product differentiation gives ∂_j g_j=(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}+u_t·u_{x_jx_j}, where each dot product is the finite sum over m Euclidean components. For fixed other coordinates y, z↦g_j(x(y,z)) is smooth and zero for |z|>L. M002, with V=ℝ, endpoints −L,L and f(z)=g_j(x(y,z)), applies: ℝ is complete, the derivative follows from smoothness, and the continuous derivative is interval-integrable on the compact interval. Its endpoint values vanish, so the interval integral of the derivative is zero; the derivative vanishes outside the interval as well, hence its whole-line integral is zero. For n=1 the other-coordinate space is ℝ⁰, a singleton. The coordinate-permuted derivative is continuous compactly supported on ℝⁿ, thus product-integrable by M001 before M003 is used. Euclidean coordinate permutation preserves Lebesgue measure: it permutes the orthonormal coordinate basis and preserves the volumes of boxes, which generate Borel Lebesgue measure. The factor Lebesgue measures are σ-finite, hence S-finite, and ℝ is real normed; therefore M003 applies and identifies the whole-space integral with the iterated integral, which is zero by the one-dimensional calculation. It follows that ∫∂_j g_j=0 and ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}=−∫u_t·u_{x_jx_j}. Every such whole-space field is integrable by M001. Differentiate the exact Frobenius energy definition: e_t=2u_t·u_{tt}+2Σ_j u_{x_j}·∂_j u_t. Finite sums commute with integration. Summing the integration-by-parts identities and using Δ_xu=Σ_j u_{x_jx_j} gives E'(r)=2∫u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu). Differentiating H001 gives 2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. At the same point H002 supplies a scalar a with u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a u. Taking the inner product with u_t gives u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(u_t·u)=0. The zero integrand is integrable; thus E'(r)=0. No regularity of a is used, and arbitrary r>0 was fixed.\\\"}]}\"}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":1111,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_031097c1e2840610016adb39536698819196cacb921ae8377f","call_id":"call_41d3881bd18a4c85a7ca8fbf0280f5e0","name":"submit_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_input\\\":\\\"prior-candidate.json\\\",\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Fix r>0, J=[r/2,3r/2], and choose compact C from H003. For every s∈J, u(·,s)=q on the open complement of C. Smoothness implies every first and higher derivative in x and t vanishes there; consequently e and e_t at each fixed s are continuous and supported in C, hence compactly supported. M001 applies with X=ℝⁿ, Lebesgue measure and V=ℝ, since Euclidean opens are Borel measurable and Lebesgue measure is finite on compact sets. Thus e(·,s), e_t(·,s), and all continuous compactly supported scalar fields below are integrable; E(s) is the ordinary finite Lebesgue integral. C×J is compact by M005, and e_t is continuous on it, so M004 gives uniform continuity. Apply M006 with α=ℝⁿ, μ=Lebesgue, 𝕜=ℝ, E=ℝ, x₀=r, s=(r/2,3r/2), F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ), F'(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ). The neighborhood inclusion holds; F(τ) is strongly measurable because it is continuous, and F(r) integrable by M001; F'(r) is strongly measurable. Continuity on compact C×J gives a finite bound K for |e_t| there. Set b=K·1_C; C is compact measurable of finite measure, so b is integrable. Outside C the derivative vanishes, and on C it is bounded by K; hence |F'(τ)(x)|≤b(x) for τ∈s. Smoothness gives the required HasDerivAt in τ for every x and τ. All M006 hypotheses are now checked, yielding E differentiable at r and E'(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx. For the later coordinate argument choose L bounding C: each coordinate projection of C is compact and therefore bounded; the finitely many coordinate bounds have a common finite maximum. The box [-L,L]^n contains C. C and J compact give C×J compact by M005; these are proved compactness facts, not properties granted by the witness declarations.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"For fixed r and each j define g_j(x)=u_t(x,r)·u_{x_j}(x,r). The factors and their derivatives vanish outside C, so g_j and ∂_j g_j are smooth and compactly supported; M001 proves their integrability. Product differentiation gives ∂_j g_j=(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}+u_t·u_{x_jx_j}, where each dot product is the finite sum over m Euclidean components. For fixed other coordinates y, z↦g_j(x(y,z)) is smooth and zero for |z|>L. M002, with V=ℝ, endpoints −L,L and f(z)=g_j(x(y,z)), applies: ℝ is complete, the derivative follows from smoothness, and the continuous derivative is interval-integrable on the compact interval. Its endpoint values vanish, so the interval integral of the derivative is zero; the derivative vanishes outside the interval as well, hence its whole-line integral is zero. For n=1 the other-coordinate space is ℝ⁰, a singleton. The coordinate-permuted derivative is continuous compactly supported on ℝⁿ, thus product-integrable by M001 before M003 is used. Euclidean coordinate permutation preserves Lebesgue measure: it permutes the orthonormal coordinate basis and preserves the volumes of boxes, which generate Borel Lebesgue measure. The factor Lebesgue measures are σ-finite, hence S-finite, and ℝ is real normed; therefore M003 applies and identifies the whole-space integral with the iterated integral, which is zero by the one-dimensional calculation. It follows that ∫∂_j g_j=0 and ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}=−∫u_t·u_{x_jx_j}. Every such whole-space field is integrable by M001. Differentiate the exact Frobenius energy definition: e_t=2u_t·u_{tt}+2Σ_j u_{x_j}·∂_j u_t. Finite sums commute with integration. Summing the integration-by-parts identities and using Δ_xu=Σ_j u_{x_jx_j} gives E'(r)=2∫u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu). Differentiating H001 gives 2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. At the same point H002 supplies a scalar a with u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a u. Taking the inner product with u_t gives u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(u_t·u)=0. The zero integrand is integrable; thus E'(r)=0. No regularity of a is used, and arbitrary r>0 was fixed.\\\"}]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

